{"name":"Manifest Destiny","hex":"#8a9bc2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Manifest Destiny","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of manifest destiny."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Manifest_Destiny"}],"entries":[{"title":"Manifest destiny","blocks":[{"text":"Manifest destiny was the expansionist colonial belief in the 19th-century United States that American settlers were destined to expand westward across North America, and that this belief was both obvious (\"manifest\") and certain (\"destiny\"). The belief is rooted in American exceptionalism, romantic nationalism, Anglo-Saxonism, and nascent ideas of white chauvinism, implying the inevitable spread of republicanism and the American way. It is one of the earliest expressions of American imperialism. According to historian William Earl Weeks, there were three basic tenets behind the concept: The assumption of the unique moral virtue of the United States, the assertion of its mission to redeem the world by the spread of republican government and more generally the \"American way of life\", and the faith in the nation's divinely ordained destiny to succeed in this mission."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/American_Progress_%28John_Gast_painting%29.jpg/330px-American_Progress_%28John_Gast_painting%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manifest_destiny","wikidata":"Q474184"},{"title":"Manifest Destiny (opera)","blocks":[{"text":"Manifest Destiny is an opera composed by Keith Burstein with an English libretto by Dic Edwards. The opera is notable for dealing with the subject of Islamic suicide bombers, and with the ramifications of both the Middle Eastern conflict and the war on terror."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manifest_Destiny_(opera)","wikidata":"Q6749519"},{"title":"Destiny","blocks":[{"text":"Destiny, sometimes also called fate, is a predetermined course of events."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Alphonse_Mucha_-_Fate.jpg/330px-Alphonse_Mucha_-_Fate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Destiny","wikidata":"Q216774"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/John_O%27Sullivan.jpg/500px-John_O%27Sullivan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/John_O%27Sullivan.jpg/500px-John_O%27Sullivan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John L. O'Sullivan, sketched in 1874, was an influential columnist as a young man, but he is now generally remembered only for his use of the phrase \"manifest destiny\" to advocate the annexation of Texas and Oregon.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_O'Sullivan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/John_Quincy_Adams.jpeg/500px-John_Quincy_Adams.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/John_Quincy_Adams.jpeg/1280px-John_Quincy_Adams.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/John_Quincy_Adams.jpeg/500px-John_Quincy_Adams.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John Quincy Adams, painted above in 1816 by Charles Robert Leslie, was an early proponent of continentalism. Late in life, he came to regret his role in helping U.S. slavery to expand, and became a leading opponent of the annexation of Texas.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Quincy_Adams.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Alfred_Jacob_Miller_-_Fort_Laramie_-_Walters_37194049.jpg/500px-Alfred_Jacob_Miller_-_Fort_Laramie_-_Walters_37194049.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Alfred_Jacob_Miller_-_Fort_Laramie_-_Walters_37194049.jpg/1280px-Alfred_Jacob_Miller_-_Fort_Laramie_-_Walters_37194049.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Alfred_Jacob_Miller_-_Fort_Laramie_-_Walters_37194049.jpg/500px-Alfred_Jacob_Miller_-_Fort_Laramie_-_Walters_37194049.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first Fort Laramie as it looked prior to 1840. Painting from memory by Alfred Jacob Miller","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlfred_Jacob_Miller_-_Fort_Laramie_-_Walters_37194049.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Emanuel_Leutze_-_Westward_the_Course_of_Empire_Takes_Its_Way_-_Smithsonian.jpg/500px-Emanuel_Leutze_-_Westward_the_Course_of_Empire_Takes_Its_Way_-_Smithsonian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Emanuel_Leutze_-_Westward_the_Course_of_Empire_Takes_Its_Way_-_Smithsonian.jpg/1280px-Emanuel_Leutze_-_Westward_the_Course_of_Empire_Takes_Its_Way_-_Smithsonian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Emanuel_Leutze_-_Westward_the_Course_of_Empire_Takes_Its_Way_-_Smithsonian.jpg/500px-Emanuel_Leutze_-_Westward_the_Course_of_Empire_Takes_Its_Way_-_Smithsonian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"American westward expansion is idealized in Emanuel Leutze's famous painting Westward the Course of Empire Takes Its Way (1861).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEmanuel_Leutze_-_Westward_the_Course_of_Empir